# Build outputs (make all / make iso) -- all regenerable from source
/build/
/iso/
/toy-os.iso
# The LIVE and DEMO ISOs and their staging trees (make live-iso /
# make demo-iso). These arrived after the three lines above and were
# never added beside them, which is how ~84 MB of build output --
# toy-os-live.iso, iso-live/boot/live.img and iso-live/boot/kernel.bin
# -- got committed by a `git add -A` after a `make live-iso`. Nothing
# warned: the staging directories only exist once you have built that
# target, so an ignore gap here is invisible until someone does.
#
# If a THIRD ISO target is ever added, add its artifact and its staging
# tree here in the same commit.
/iso-live/
/iso-demo/
/toy-os-live.iso
/toy-os-demo.iso

# GENERATED by tools/gen_version.sh as the first step of every build --
# never hand-edited (the file says so itself), and since it carries the
# build's commit id and dirty flag it now changes on every commit and on
# the first build after any edit. Tracking a file like that means every
# pull stops with "your local changes would be overwritten", which is
# exactly what happened the day the build id landed. Regenerated before
# anything that includes it compiles, so its absence from a fresh clone
# is a non-event.
/kernel/include/api/version.h
/kernel/include/api/build_date.h

# Persistent disk image (see Makefile's DISK_IMG comment) -- deliberately
# left alone by `make clean`, it's local dev state, not a repo asset.
/disk.img
# Release assets, built by hand when cutting a release (see
# docs/decisions.md's versioning entry): the gzipped disk image that
# gets attached to a GitHub Release, and the checksum file covering the
# uploaded set. Both are reproducible from a build, and disk.img.gz in
# particular is ~10MB of binary that has no business in git history.
/disk.img.gz
/SHA256SUMS
# Pre-Btrfs-+C-fix backup of a real disk.img, left behind by the
# no-COW swap described in the git history -- same "not a repo asset"
# reasoning as disk.img itself, just not matched by the exact pattern
# above. Safe to delete by hand once the swap's confirmed good.
/disk.img.cow.bak

# tools/tfs2_writer.py seed staging tree -- the Makefile's `seed` target
# stages built ELFs (e.g. $(LSPCI_ELF)) under seed/sync/ fresh every
# build; it's a regenerable copy of a build output, not a source file.
# A future seed/once/ (hand-authored config seed files) would NOT be
# ignored -- only add an entry here if once/ ever needs one too.
/seed/sync/

# QEMU/QMP test scratch, if run from the repo root (see tools/qmp_test.py)
*.ppm
qemu.log
serial.log

# tools/boot_smoke_test.py's default log filenames (its own --serial-log/
# --qemu-log defaults) -- same reasoning as qemu.log/serial.log above,
# just named differently so a smoke-test run and a QMP-test run left
# side by side don't clobber each other's logs.
smoke_serial.log
smoke_qemu.log

# Python bytecode from tools/*.py
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Scratch holding area for files being removed from the device-bridge
# workflow (see CLAUDE.md's "device bridge can't delete files" note) --
# never a real repo asset, just a staging spot the user empties by hand.
/_to_delete/

# Delivery workaround for Makefile (and .github/workflows/*.yml) being
# protected paths the device bridge won't write to directly (see
# CLAUDE.md's "Makefile and anything under .github/workflows/*.yml are
# protected files" note) -- transient hand-off artifacts the user
# copies into place and discards, not repo assets either. The workflow
# one has no fixed name (whatever avoids the protected path, e.g.
# build.yml.new) -- *.yml.new covers any of them.
/Makefile.new
*.yml.new

# tools/vm.py's managed VM: pidfile + COM1 unix socket. The wildcard
# forms are `vm.py --instance N`'s (.vm.1.pid, .vm.1.serial, ...), which
# tools/damage_hunt.py leaves one of per parallel slot.
.vm.pid
.vm.serial
.vm.*.pid
.vm.*.serial
ktest_qemu.log

# Claude Code worktrees (.claude/worktrees/) are transient checkouts, not
# repo content -- the skill beside them IS tracked, so this ignores the
# one directory rather than all of .claude/.
/.claude/worktrees/

# tools/qemu_matrix.py's scratch: copied disk images and QEMU logs.
.qemu_matrix/

# tools/hires_test.py's screendumps (its default --shots directory).
.hires_shots/

# tools/virtio_boot_test.py's working image and log (it copies disk.img
# rather than writing to it, and the copy is disposable), and the second
# disk image CI's virtio job makes.
virtio_boot.img
virtio_boot_qemu.log
ci-virtio.img

# clangd's compilation database, generated by `bear -- make all`.
# Machine-specific absolute paths, so it is regenerated rather than
# shared -- same reasoning as the generated version.h/build_date.h.
/compile_commands.json
/.cache/clangd/

# The Doom IWAD. FETCHED, NOT COMMITTED -- `tools/fetch_wad.py` puts it
# here. id's shareware licence permits distributing the unmodified
# shareware PACKAGE; lifting one file out of it into an unrelated
# project's git history is a different act, and not one this repo needs
# to perform to work. See docs/decisions.md.
/data/doom/
/seed/sync/usr/share/doom/

# Work images written by tools/partition_test.py. It defaults to a temp
# directory now, but an explicit --work in the repo would still land
# here, and these are GIGABYTES -- one `git add -A` away from a commit
# GitHub rejects outright (it did).
/partition_boot*.img
/partition_boot_qemu.log
